export const G6={title:'Molecular genetics: DNA, RNA and protein',hook:'A single change in one DNA base—an A replaced by a T—changes one amino acid in hemoglobin and causes sickle cell disease. Molecular genetics (15% of the test) explains how DNA stores information, copies itself and directs the making of proteins, and how mutations change the message.',objectives:['Describe the structure of DNA and the discoveries that revealed it.','Explain semiconservative DNA replication and the enzymes involved.','Describe transcription, RNA processing and translation.','Classify mutations and explain how genes are regulated.'],sections:[
['The structure of DNA',[
'DNA is a polymer of **nucleotides**, each made of a **phosphate group**, the sugar **deoxyribose** and one of four **nitrogenous bases**: **adenine (A)** and **guanine (G)**, the double-ringed **purines**, and **cytosine (C)** and **thymine (T)**, the single-ringed **pyrimidines**. **Erwin Chargaff** found that A equals T and G equals C in amount (**Chargaff’s rules**). **Rosalind Franklin’s** X-ray diffraction images, including **Photo 51**, and work with Maurice Wilkins revealed a helix; **James Watson** and **Francis Crick** built the **double helix** model in **1953**. Two **antiparallel** strands (one running 5′→3′, the other 3′→5′) have sugar-phosphate backbones on the outside and **complementary base pairs** inside: **A–T** with two hydrogen bonds and **G–C** with three. RNA differs: it is usually single-stranded, uses **ribose** and has **uracil (U)** instead of thymine.',
sort('DNA, RNA or both?',['DNA only','RNA only','Both'],[['Deoxyribose sugar',0],['Thymine',0],['Uracil',1],['Ribose sugar',1],['Adenine, guanine and cytosine',2],['Phosphate backbone',2],['Usually double-stranded',0]])]],
['DNA replication',[
'DNA replicates **semiconservatively**: each new double helix has **one original strand and one new strand**, as Meselson and Stahl demonstrated with heavy nitrogen. **Helicase** unwinds and separates the strands at the **replication fork**, **topoisomerase** relieves twisting ahead of the fork, and single-strand binding proteins keep strands apart. **Primase** lays down a short **RNA primer**, and **DNA polymerase** adds nucleotides only to the **3′ end**, so new DNA grows **5′→3′**. The **leading strand** is made continuously toward the fork; the **lagging strand** is made in short **Okazaki fragments** away from the fork. Another DNA polymerase replaces the primers with DNA, and **DNA ligase** seals the gaps. DNA polymerase also **proofreads**, keeping errors to roughly one in a billion bases.',
order('Steps of DNA replication',['Helicase unwinds and separates the strands','Primase adds an RNA primer','DNA polymerase extends new strands 5′→3′','The lagging strand forms as Okazaki fragments','Primers are replaced with DNA','DNA ligase seals the fragments'])]],
['Transcription and RNA processing',[
'The **central dogma** describes information flow: **DNA → RNA → protein**. In **transcription**, **RNA polymerase** binds a **promoter** and reads the **template strand** 3′→5′, building complementary **messenger RNA (mRNA)** 5′→3′, with U pairing with A. In eukaryotes, the **pre-mRNA** is processed in the nucleus: a **5′ cap** and a **poly-A tail** protect it and help it leave the nucleus and be translated, and **introns** (noncoding sequences) are removed by the **spliceosome** while **exons** are joined—**alternative splicing** lets one gene make several proteins. Other RNAs include **transfer RNA (tRNA)**, which carries amino acids, and **ribosomal RNA (rRNA)**, which forms ribosomes with proteins.',
match('Match the molecule to its role',[['RNA polymerase','Builds mRNA from the DNA template'],['Promoter','DNA sequence where transcription starts'],['Intron','Noncoding sequence removed from pre-mRNA'],['Exon','Coding sequence kept in mature mRNA'],['tRNA','Carries an amino acid and has an anticodon'],['rRNA','Structural and catalytic part of the ribosome']])]],
['Translation and the genetic code',[
'In **translation**, a **ribosome** reads mRNA in three-base **codons**. Each **tRNA** has an **anticodon** that pairs with a codon and carries the matching amino acid. Translation starts at the **start codon AUG** (methionine), elongates as the ribosome moves along the mRNA—tRNAs pass through the **A, P and E sites** while peptide bonds link amino acids—and ends at a **stop codon (UAA, UAG or UGA)**. The genetic code is **redundant** (most amino acids have several codons), **unambiguous** (each codon means one thing) and nearly **universal** across life, which is why bacteria can make human insulin. Practice transcribing and translating below.',
{t:'w',kind:'codonlab',title:'From DNA to protein',prompt:'Write the mRNA for each template strand, read the protein and then classify the mutation.'}]],
['Mutations and gene regulation',[
'A **mutation** is a change in DNA. **Point mutations (substitutions)** change one base: a **silent** mutation codes the same amino acid; a **missense** mutation changes one amino acid (as in sickle cell disease); a **nonsense** mutation creates a premature stop codon. **Insertions and deletions** of bases not in multiples of three cause **frameshift** mutations that change every codon after the change. Mutations arise from replication errors or **mutagens** such as UV light, radiation and certain chemicals; they are the source of new alleles. Cells **regulate** which genes are expressed: in bacteria, the ***lac* operon** is **inducible**—a **repressor** blocks transcription until lactose is present—and the *trp* operon is **repressible**. Eukaryotes regulate genes through transcription factors, chromatin packing and **epigenetic** changes such as **DNA methylation**, which can turn genes off without changing the sequence.',
sort('Classify the mutation',['Point mutation','Frameshift mutation'],[['One base replaced by another',0],['A silent change in a codon',0],['Insertion of one base',1],['Deletion of two bases',1],['A codon changed to UAG (nonsense)',0],['Every codon after the change is altered',1]]),
kase('Reading a mutation',{a:{text:'A DNA strand changes from TAC GGA TTC to TAC GGA ATC. What type of mutation is this?',choices:[['A substitution (point mutation)','b','Correct. One base was replaced; the length is unchanged.',true],['A frameshift deletion','b','No bases were lost.',false]]},b:{text:'Another strand changes from TAC GGA TTC to TAC GAT TC. What happened?',choices:[['A deletion causing a frameshift','c','Correct. One G was deleted, shifting the reading frame.',true],['A silent substitution','c','The length changed, so the frame shifted.',false]]},c:{text:'Frameshifts usually change many amino acids and often create early stop codons, so they are typically more harmful than single substitutions.',end:true}})]],
["DNA damage and repair",["Cells constantly repair DNA. **Proofreading** by DNA polymerase fixes most copying errors immediately; **mismatch repair** enzymes find and correct mispaired bases that slip through; **base excision repair** removes damaged single bases; **nucleotide excision repair** cuts out bulky damage such as **thymine dimers** caused by **ultraviolet (UV) light** and fills the gap; and **double-strand break repair** rejoins broken chromosomes. People with **xeroderma pigmentosum** lack a nucleotide excision repair enzyme, so UV damage builds up and their risk of skin cancer is extremely high. Mutations in repair genes such as ***BRCA1*** and ***BRCA2*** also raise cancer risk.","**Mutagens** include UV light, X-rays and gamma radiation, tobacco smoke chemicals and some viruses. The **Ames test** uses bacteria to screen chemicals for their ability to cause mutations. Mutations in **somatic** (body) cells can lead to cancer but are not inherited; mutations in **germ-line** cells (eggs and sperm) can be passed to offspring and are the source of new inherited variation.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the repair process or mutagen to its description","pairs":[["Proofreading","DNA polymerase checks and corrects new bases"],["Mismatch repair","Fixes mispaired bases after replication"],["Nucleotide excision repair","Removes thymine dimers from UV light"],["Xeroderma pigmentosum","Inherited loss of UV damage repair"],["Ames test","Bacterial screen for mutagenic chemicals"],["Germ-line mutation","Can be passed to offspring"]]}]]
],takeaways:['DNA is a double helix of antiparallel strands with A–T and G–C pairs (Watson, Crick, Franklin, Chargaff); RNA has ribose and uracil.','Replication is semiconservative: helicase, primase, DNA polymerase (5′→3′, leading and Okazaki fragments) and ligase.','DNA → mRNA (RNA polymerase, splicing) → protein (codons, tRNA anticodons, AUG start, stop codons); substitutions are silent, missense or nonsense; insertions/deletions cause frameshifts.'],terms:[['Codon','Three-base mRNA sequence specifying an amino acid or stop.'],['Semiconservative replication','Each new DNA molecule keeps one original strand.'],['Frameshift mutation','Insertion or deletion that shifts the reading frame.']],mcqs:[q('Which base pairs with adenine in DNA?','Thymine','Uracil','Guanine','Cytosine','A–T and G–C.'),q('DNA replication is described as:','Semiconservative','Conservative','Dispersive','Random','Each new helix keeps one old strand.'),q('Which enzyme seals Okazaki fragments?','DNA ligase','Helicase','Primase','RNA polymerase','Ligase joins fragments.'),q('The start codon is:','AUG','UAA','UGA','UAG','It codes methionine.'),q('Introns are:','Removed from pre-mRNA','Translated into protein','Added to mRNA','Found only in bacteria','Exons are kept.'),q('A deletion of one base usually causes:','A frameshift mutation','A silent mutation','No change','A translocation','The reading frame shifts.'),q('Which scientist produced Photo 51?','Rosalind Franklin','Gregor Mendel','Thomas Hunt Morgan','Erwin Chargaff','Her X-ray images revealed the helix.'),q('The lac operon is:','Inducible—turned on when lactose is present','Always on','Repressible by tryptophan','Found only in humans','A repressor blocks it until lactose binds.')],frq:{prompt:'Trace the path from a gene to a protein in a eukaryotic cell, and explain how a substitution and a single-base deletion would each affect the protein.',rubric:[['Transcription',['rna polymerase','promoter','template','mrna','complementary']],['RNA processing',['cap','poly-a','intron','exon','splic']],['Translation',['ribosome','codon','trna','anticodon','aug','stop']],['Mutation effects',['substitution','silent','missense','nonsense','frameshift','deletion']]],model:'In the nucleus, RNA polymerase binds the promoter and reads the template strand 3′ to 5′, building complementary pre-mRNA with uracil in place of thymine. The pre-mRNA receives a 5′ cap and a poly-A tail, and the spliceosome removes introns and joins exons to make mature mRNA, which leaves the nucleus. In the cytoplasm, a ribosome reads the mRNA in codons, beginning at the start codon AUG; tRNAs with complementary anticodons bring the matching amino acids, which are joined by peptide bonds until a stop codon releases the finished polypeptide. A substitution changes one base and therefore at most one codon: if the new codon codes the same amino acid it is silent, if it codes a different amino acid it is missense (as in sickle cell disease), and if it becomes a stop codon it is nonsense and shortens the protein. A single-base deletion shifts the reading frame, so every codon after it changes, usually producing a completely different, often truncated and nonfunctional protein.'},sources:[RULES,OS_DNA,OS_REP,OS_CODE,OS_TRANSL]};

export const G7={title:'Experimental design, model organisms and DNA extraction (Skill I)',hook:'Why study fruit flies to understand human disease? Because flies, bacteria, yeast and fast-growing plants share genes and processes with us and reproduce quickly. Experimental design and model organisms make up 15% of the test, and Skill I—DNA extraction—is the first hands-on step in molecular genetics.',objectives:['Design a controlled genetics experiment with testable hypotheses.','Identify independent, dependent and controlled variables and appropriate controls.','Explain why model organisms are used and compare common examples.','Perform DNA extraction following HOSA Skill I and explain the chemistry of each step.'],sections:[
['Designing a genetics experiment',[
'Science begins with a **question** and a **hypothesis**—a **testable, falsifiable** prediction, such as “If the white-eye allele is X-linked, then crossing white-eyed females with red-eyed males will produce white-eyed sons and red-eyed daughters.” Scientists also state a **null hypothesis (H₀)**, which predicts no effect or no difference—for example, “There is no significant difference between the observed offspring counts and the counts expected for a 3:1 ratio.” The **independent variable** is what the experimenter changes (such as the parental cross); the **dependent variable** is what is measured (offspring phenotypes); **controlled variables** are kept the same (temperature, food, light). A **control group** provides a baseline: a **negative control** should show no effect, and a **positive control** should show a known effect. **Replication** and adequate **sample size** make results reliable, and data are recorded carefully and analyzed statistically.',
sort('Identify the variable in a Fast Plants experiment testing whether light color changes stem color',['Independent variable','Dependent variable','Controlled variable'],[['Color of light the plants receive',0],['Number of plants with purple stems',1],['Type of soil',2],['Amount of water',2],['Temperature of the growing area',2],['Stem color scored at day 7',1]]),
chk('Which is a falsifiable hypothesis?',['If the trait is autosomal recessive, then a cross of two carriers will give about 3 dominant to 1 recessive offspring','Genetics is interesting','Fruit flies are the best organism','Traits come from somewhere'],'A hypothesis makes a testable prediction that could be shown wrong.')]],
['Why model organisms?',[
'**Model organisms** are species studied in depth because discoveries in them apply to many others. Good models have a **short generation time**, produce **many offspring**, are **small and inexpensive** to keep, have **easily observed traits**, a **sequenced genome** and tools for genetic manipulation, and raise fewer ethical concerns than studying humans. Because genes such as those controlling cell division and development are **conserved** across species, research in flies, worms and yeast has led to major medical discoveries.',
match('Match the model organism to its strength',[['Escherichia coli','Divides about every 20 minutes; used for gene cloning'],['Saccharomyces cerevisiae (yeast)','Simple eukaryote for cell cycle and aging research'],['Drosophila melanogaster','About 10–12 day life cycle at 25 °C, four chromosome pairs'],['Caenorhabditis elegans','Transparent worm with a mapped cell lineage'],['Wisconsin Fast Plants (Brassica rapa)','Seed-to-seed in about 5–6 weeks under continuous light'],['Mouse (Mus musculus)','Mammal for modeling human disease']])]],
['Genetics model organisms in HOSA skills',[
'*Drosophila melanogaster*, the fruit fly, has visible mutations in eye color, wing shape and body color, males and females that are easy to tell apart and a life cycle of egg, larva (three instars), pupa and adult. **Wisconsin Fast Plants** (*Brassica rapa*), developed at the University of Wisconsin–Madison, flower within about two weeks, are **self-incompatible** (they must be cross-pollinated) and show traits such as stem color and leaf color. **Genetic corn** ears show kernel colors and textures that can be counted for ratios. ***E. coli*** strains with antibiotic resistance markers show gene transfer. Knowing each organism’s life cycle, traits and handling makes Round Two skills faster and safer.',
flip('Model organism quick facts',[['Fruit fly sexing','Males smaller with dark, rounded abdomen tips and sex combs; females larger with pointed, striped abdomens'],['Fast Plants pollination','Must transfer pollen between different plants'],['Genetic corn','Count kernel phenotypes on an ear for chi-square'],['E. coli markers','Antibiotic resistance genes allow selection'],['Yeast','Single-celled eukaryote; can be haploid or diploid'],['Zebrafish','Transparent embryos for watching development']])]],
['DNA extraction chemistry',[
'DNA can be extracted from almost any cells using three ideas. **Breaking cells open (lysis)**: mashing physically breaks tissue, and the **detergent** in the extraction buffer dissolves the **lipid membranes** of the cell and nucleus. **Separating DNA from other material**: **salt** in the buffer neutralizes the negative charges on DNA’s phosphate backbone and helps proteins separate, so DNA strands can clump; **filtering** removes cell debris. **Precipitating DNA**: DNA is not soluble in **cold ethanol**, so when ice-cold 95% ethanol is layered on top of the filtrate, DNA comes out of solution at the boundary as white, stringy threads that can be **spooled** onto a stick. Cold temperatures also slow enzymes that break down DNA. Strawberries are a popular source because ripe fruit is soft and the plants are **octoploid** (eight sets of chromosomes), so they contain a lot of DNA.',
match('Match the ingredient or step to its purpose',[['Mashing in a bag','Physically breaks tissue and cells'],['Detergent','Dissolves cell and nuclear membranes'],['Salt','Neutralizes DNA’s charge so strands clump'],['Cheesecloth filter','Removes cell debris'],['Cold 95% ethanol','Makes DNA precipitate'],['Wooden stick','Spools the DNA threads']])]],
['Performing DNA extraction (HOSA Skill I)',[
'In Skill I (20 minutes), the competitor enters the testing area in **closed-toed shoes** and dons **safety glasses or goggles and gloves** (a lab coat is optional). They place the **DNA source** in a bag, seal it and **gently mash with their hands for 2 minutes**; measure **10 mL of extraction buffer**, pour it into the bag and **mash for one additional minute**; place a **funnel in a 50-mL centrifuge tube** and line it with folded **cheesecloth** to create a filter; and **pour the mixture through the cheesecloth** into the 50-mL tube. They carefully pour **2 mL of the filtered extract into a clean 15-mL tube**, use a transfer pipette to add **5 mL of chilled 95% ethanol**, then insert and **rotate a wooden stick or transfer pipette in the ethanol just above the extract layer** to wind (spool) the DNA, and **transfer the DNA into a 1.5-mL tube**. Finally, they dispose of bags, cheesecloth and used tubes, **clean the work surface with disinfectant** and **remove PPE** (20 points; 70% is 14).',
order('DNA extraction (HOSA Skill I)',['Closed-toed shoes; put on goggles and gloves','Seal the fruit in a bag and mash for 2 minutes','Add 10 mL extraction buffer and mash 1 more minute','Line a funnel in a 50-mL tube with cheesecloth','Filter the mixture into the 50-mL tube','Pour 2 mL of filtrate into a 15-mL tube','Add 5 mL chilled 95% ethanol with a transfer pipette','Spool DNA above the extract layer and transfer it to a 1.5-mL tube','Dispose of materials, disinfect the surface and remove PPE']),
kase('Troubleshooting an extraction',{a:{text:'After adding ethanol, you see very little DNA. Which step most likely went wrong?',choices:[['The ethanol was not chilled, or the extract was mixed with the ethanol instead of layered','b','Correct. Cold ethanol layered gently on top gives the best precipitate.',true],['You wore goggles','b','PPE does not affect yield.',false]]},b:{text:'Your filtrate is full of pulp. What should you do next time?',choices:[['Use folded cheesecloth in the funnel and pour slowly','c','Correct. A good filter removes debris.',true],['Skip filtering to save time','c','Debris interferes with seeing and spooling DNA.',false]]},c:{text:'With chilled ethanol layered gently on clean filtrate, white strands form at the boundary and spool easily.',end:true}})]],
["Laboratory safety, measurement and record keeping",["Every Genetics skill begins and ends with safety: **closed-toed shoes**, **goggles and gloves** (and other PPE the guidelines list), no food or drink, tied-back hair and a clean, disinfected work surface. Classroom *E. coli* strains such as K-12 derivatives are **Biosafety Level 1 (BSL-1)** organisms—not known to cause disease in healthy adults—but are still handled with sterile technique, and cultures and contaminated materials are disposed of as **biohazard waste** (often by autoclaving or bleach). Ethanol is **flammable**, so it is kept away from flames. Report spills and injuries immediately.","Accurate measurement matters: **1 mL = 1,000 µL**. Read graduated tubes and cylinders at the bottom of the **meniscus** at eye level, use the measuring cup provided for Drosophila medium (one level measure), and use **transfer pipettes** or fixed-volume devices for small volumes as the rating sheets allow. Scientists keep a **lab notebook** with the date, procedure, observations, data tables and any changes or errors, written in ink. Clear labels—sample, condition, date and initials, as Skills II and III require—make results traceable.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Safe and accurate practice?","buckets":["Correct","Incorrect"],"items":[["Reading volume at the bottom of the meniscus at eye level",0],["Keeping ethanol away from open flames",0],["Wearing sandals in the lab",1],["Labeling tubes with condition, date and initials",0],["Pouring used cultures down the sink untreated",1],["Recording observations in ink in a lab notebook",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['A hypothesis is a falsifiable prediction; the null hypothesis predicts no difference; identify independent, dependent and controlled variables and use controls.','Model organisms (E. coli, yeast, Drosophila, C. elegans, Fast Plants, mice) have short generations, many offspring and conserved genes.','Skill I: mash 2 min, 10 mL buffer and mash 1 min, filter through cheesecloth, 2 mL filtrate + 5 mL chilled 95% ethanol, spool and transfer to a 1.5-mL tube; detergent lyses, salt clumps DNA, cold ethanol precipitates it.'],terms:[['Null hypothesis','Prediction of no effect or no difference.'],['Model organism','Species studied in depth to understand biology broadly.'],['Precipitate','To come out of solution as a solid.']],mcqs:[q('In Skill I, how much extraction buffer is added?','10 mL','2 mL','5 mL','50 mL','Then mash for one more minute.'),q('What makes DNA precipitate?','Cold 95% ethanol','Warm water','Detergent','Salt alone','DNA is insoluble in cold ethanol.'),q('The detergent in extraction buffer:','Dissolves cell and nuclear membranes','Cuts DNA into pieces','Colors the DNA','Precipitates DNA','Membranes are made of lipids.'),q('Which is a good feature of a model organism?','Short generation time and many offspring','Very long life span','Few offspring','No known genome','These speed genetic studies.'),q('In a cross testing a 3:1 ratio, the null hypothesis states:','There is no significant difference between observed and expected counts','The ratio is definitely 3:1','The experiment failed','Variables were not controlled','Chi-square tests the null.'),q('In a Fast Plants experiment varying light color, the light color is the:','Independent variable','Dependent variable','Controlled variable','Control group','It is what the experimenter changes.'),q('How much chilled ethanol is added to the 2 mL of filtrate in Skill I?','5 mL','10 mL','1 mL','50 mL','Add with a transfer pipette.'),q('Why are strawberries a good DNA source?','They are soft and octoploid, with lots of DNA','They have no DNA','They are haploid','They are bacteria','Eight chromosome sets.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain each step of HOSA Skill I (DNA extraction) and the scientific reason for it, then describe how you would design a controlled experiment to test whether the amount of salt in the buffer affects DNA yield.',rubric:[['Lysis steps and reasons',['mash','2 minutes','detergent','membrane','10 ml','buffer']],['Filtering and precipitation',['cheesecloth','filter','2 ml','5 ml','ethanol','cold','spool']],['Safety and cleanup',['closed-toed','goggles','gloves','disinfect','ppe']],['Experimental design',['independent','dependent','controlled','hypothesis','null','control','replicat']]],model:'After entering in closed-toed shoes and putting on goggles and gloves, I would seal the fruit in a bag and mash it for 2 minutes to break the tissue and cells, then add 10 mL of extraction buffer and mash another minute: the detergent dissolves the lipid cell and nuclear membranes, and the salt neutralizes DNA’s negative charges so strands clump and proteins separate. I would line a funnel in a 50-mL tube with cheesecloth and filter the mixture to remove debris, pour 2 mL of filtrate into a 15-mL tube and gently add 5 mL of chilled 95% ethanol, in which DNA is insoluble, so it precipitates at the boundary; I would spool it with a wooden stick and transfer it to a 1.5-mL tube, then dispose of materials, disinfect the surface and remove PPE. To test salt, my hypothesis would be: if salt helps DNA clump, then buffers with more salt will yield more DNA, and my null hypothesis that salt concentration has no effect on yield. The independent variable is salt concentration (for example 0, 1 and 2 grams per 100 mL), the dependent variable is the mass of spooled DNA, and controlled variables include fruit mass, ripeness, mashing time, ethanol temperature and volumes. The buffer without salt is a comparison control, and I would run several replicates of each condition and compare the averages statistically.'},sources:[RULES,CAROLINA,FASTPLANTS]};

export const G8={title:'Bacterial genetics, gene transfer and biotechnology tools (Skill II)',hook:'Bacteria reproduce asexually, yet they swap genes constantly—which is how antibiotic resistance can spread through a hospital. Skill II has competitors move genes between E. coli strains by conjugation or with a virus by transduction, using sterile technique, selective plates and serial dilutions.',objectives:['Compare transformation, conjugation and transduction.','Use sterile technique, selective media and streaking for isolation.','Perform the conjugation (II-A) and transduction (II-B) procedures and explain the controls.','Calculate serial dilutions and describe PCR, gel electrophoresis and recombinant DNA.'],sections:[
['How bacteria exchange genes',[
'Bacteria reproduce by **binary fission**, producing clones, but they gain new genes by **horizontal gene transfer**. In **transformation**, a bacterium takes up **free DNA** from its surroundings—often a **plasmid**, a small circular DNA molecule. In **conjugation**, a donor cell with an **F (fertility) factor** builds a **pilus** that contacts a recipient, and a copy of a plasmid (or part of the chromosome) passes through a connection between the cells. In **transduction**, a **bacteriophage** (a virus that infects bacteria) accidentally packages bacterial DNA and carries it into the next cell it infects. Plasmids often carry **antibiotic resistance genes**, so these processes help resistance spread. In the lab, resistance genes act as **selectable markers**: only cells that received the gene grow on a plate containing that antibiotic.',
match('Match the process to its description',[['Transformation','Uptake of free DNA from the environment'],['Conjugation','Direct transfer through a pilus between two cells'],['Transduction','Transfer of bacterial DNA by a bacteriophage'],['Binary fission','Asexual division into two identical cells'],['Plasmid','Small circular DNA that often carries resistance genes'],['Selectable marker','Gene, such as antibiotic resistance, that lets only certain cells grow']])]],
['Sterile technique, selective plates and isolation',[
'**Sterile (aseptic) technique** prevents contamination: disinfect the work surface, keep plates closed except briefly, open lids only slightly (like a clamshell), use **sterile loops and pipettes** opened from the **handle end** so the working end is never touched, never set lids face down on the bench, work quickly and dispose of used loops and tips in biohazard containers. Plates are **labeled on the bottom** (the agar side), because lids can be swapped. Plates are **incubated upside down** so condensation does not drip onto the colonies. **LB agar** supports growth of *E. coli*; adding **ampicillin (amp)** or **streptomycin (str)** makes **selective media** on which only resistant cells grow. **Streaking for isolation** spreads cells in successive sections of a plate so that single, isolated **colonies**—each from one cell—form.',
sort('Good sterile technique?',['Correct','Incorrect'],[['Opening the loop package from the handle end',0],['Lifting the plate lid only slightly',0],['Labeling plates on the bottom',0],['Setting the lid face down on the bench',1],['Touching the loop tip to check it',1],['Incubating plates upside down',0]])]],
['Bacterial conjugation (Skill II-A)',[
'In Skill II-A (20 minutes), two *E. coli* strains with different resistance markers are mated, and selective plates reveal whether genes transferred. After donning PPE, the competitor **examines the prepared confirmation plates** and records results—these show which antibiotics each strain resists on its own. To prepare the **mating plate**, they use sterile technique to dip a sterile loop in **strain I** culture and place a drop on the **LB mating plate**, use a **new loop** to place a drop of **strain II** about **½ inch away**, then use a **new sterile loop to mix** the two strains, let the liquid **soak in completely** and **invert** the plate, vocalizing “Incubate plates for 24–48 hours at room temperature (37C)” as written on the rating sheet. To test for recombination, for **each of three plates—LB+amp, LB+str and LB+str+amp**—they remove a sterile loop from its package from the handle end, slightly lift the mating plate lid to pick up bacteria and close it, streak onto the plate with it opened as little as possible and discard the loop (one point per step per plate). They tape the plates together, vocalize the incubation statement, clean and disinfect and remove PPE (30 points; 70% is 21). Growth on the **double-antibiotic** plate indicates cells that carry **both** resistance genes—evidence of gene transfer.',
kase('Interpreting conjugation plates',{a:{text:'Strain I grows on LB+amp but not LB+str; strain II grows on LB+str but not LB+amp. After mating, colonies grow on LB+str+amp. What does this show?',choices:[['Some cells now carry both resistance genes because genes transferred between strains','b','Correct. Neither parent strain alone grows on the double plate.',true],['The plates were mislabeled','b','The confirmation plates showed each strain resists only one antibiotic.',false]]},b:{text:'Why were the confirmation plates important?',choices:[['They are controls showing neither strain grows on the double plate by itself','c','Correct. Without controls, growth could not be attributed to conjugation.',true],['They provide extra food','c','Controls establish the baseline.',false]]},c:{text:'You record the results and conclude that conjugation transferred a resistance gene. (Strain markers vary by kit; follow the scenario.)',end:true}})]],
['Bacterial transduction and serial dilution (Skill II-B)',[
'Skill II-B (20 minutes) uses a **transducing lysate** (phage preparation) and two *E. coli* cultures, **BE** and **CR63**, along with **serial dilution**. A **1:10 dilution** is 1 part sample in 10 parts total (for example, 0.1 mL in 0.9 mL diluent), and a **1:100** dilution is a 1:10 of the 1:10. The competitor labels four BE tubes and four CR63 tubes **“undiluted,” “1:10,” “1:100” and “no phage,”** adds **0.5 mL** of the appropriate culture to each, then for each culture adds **10 µL** of undiluted, 1:10 and 1:100 lysate to the matching tubes—disposing of each tip or pipette in biological waste and **gently flicking** each tube to mix (one point per step per tube). They vocalize letting the tubes stand at room temperature for **5–15 minutes**. They label the bottoms of **LB/amp plates “BE”** and **LB plates “CR63”** with the date, an identifier and each condition; shake **four or five sterile glass beads** onto each lid; pour each mixture onto its plate, lifting the lid only briefly; spread with a **back-and-forth motion** (not swirling); remove the beads by holding the plate vertically and tapping them into a container through a clamshell opening; vocalize “Incubate at 37 °C for 1–2 days”; and clean up (43 points; 70% is 30.1). The **“no phage”** tubes are **negative controls**, and the dilution series shows how the amount of phage affects the result.',
{t:'example',title:'Calculating a serial dilution',prompt:'A lysate contains 2 × 10⁸ phage per mL. How many phage per mL are in the 1:10 and 1:100 dilutions, and how many phage are in a 10 µL drop of the 1:100 dilution?',steps:['1:10 dilution: 2 × 10⁸ ÷ 10 = 2 × 10⁷ per mL.','1:100 dilution: 2 × 10⁸ ÷ 100 = 2 × 10⁶ per mL.','10 µL = 0.01 mL, so 2 × 10⁶ × 0.01 = 2 × 10⁴ phage.'],answer:'2 × 10⁷/mL, 2 × 10⁶/mL and about 20,000 phage in 10 µL. (Values are invented for practice.)'},
{t:'w',kind:'mathlab',mode:'dilution',title:'Practice dilution calculations',prompt:'Use C₁V₁ = C₂V₂ to plan a dilution of a stock solution.'}]],
['Biotechnology tools',[
'The same principles power biotechnology. **Restriction enzymes** cut DNA at specific sequences, often leaving “sticky ends,” and **DNA ligase** joins pieces to make **recombinant DNA**—for example, inserting the human insulin gene into a plasmid that bacteria then express. **Transformation efficiency** measures how many cells take up a plasmid. The **polymerase chain reaction (PCR)** copies a DNA region millions of times by cycling through **denaturation (about 94–95 °C)**, **annealing** of primers (about 50–65 °C) and **extension (about 72 °C)** by heat-stable **Taq polymerase**. **Gel electrophoresis** separates DNA fragments by size: DNA is negatively charged and moves toward the **positive electrode**, and **smaller fragments travel farther**. **DNA sequencing** reads the order of bases, and **CRISPR-Cas9** edits genes at precise locations.',
{t:'w',kind:'transformation',title:'Calculate transformation efficiency',prompt:'Calculate how many transformants were produced per microgram of plasmid DNA.'},
{t:'w',kind:'gel',gel:'sizing',title:'Size DNA fragments on a gel',prompt:'Compare the unknown band with the ladder. Remember: smaller fragments travel farther.'}]]
],takeaways:['Transformation (free DNA), conjugation (pilus, F factor) and transduction (bacteriophage) move genes; antibiotic resistance genes act as selectable markers.','Sterile technique: open loops from the handle end, lift lids slightly, label plate bottoms, incubate inverted; selective media let only resistant cells grow.','II-A: mix strains on an LB mating plate and streak onto LB+amp, LB+str and LB+str+amp; II-B: 0.5 mL cultures, 10 µL undiluted/1:10/1:100 lysate, no-phage controls, glass-bead spreading.'],terms:[['Bacteriophage','Virus that infects bacteria.'],['Selective medium','Growth medium that allows only certain cells to grow.'],['Serial dilution','Stepwise dilutions, each by a set factor.']],mcqs:[q('Transfer of DNA through a pilus is:','Conjugation','Transformation','Transduction','Binary fission','It requires cell-to-cell contact.'),q('A bacteriophage transfers bacterial genes in:','Transduction','Conjugation','Transformation','Mitosis','The virus carries the DNA.'),q('Why are plates labeled on the bottom?','Lids can be swapped, but the agar side stays with the culture','It looks neater','Labels melt on the lid','Bottoms are sterile','Label the agar side.'),q('Why are plates incubated upside down?','To keep condensation from dripping on colonies','To save space','To keep them warm','It is not necessary','Condensation smears colonies.'),q('In Skill II-A, growth on LB+str+amp shows:','Cells carrying both resistance genes after transfer','Contamination only','Neither strain survived','The plate had no antibiotics','The double plate selects recombinants.'),q('A 1:100 dilution can be made by:','Diluting 1:10 twice','Adding 1 mL to 1 mL','Diluting 1:2 ten times','Adding 100 mL to 1 mL','Serial dilutions multiply.'),q('In gel electrophoresis, DNA moves toward:','The positive electrode','The negative electrode','The top of the gel only','Neither electrode','DNA is negatively charged.'),q('The “no phage” tubes in Skill II-B serve as:','Negative controls','Positive controls','Extra samples','Dilution standards','They show results without phage.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare transformation, conjugation and transduction, and explain how selective plates and controls in HOSA Skill II-A show that gene transfer occurred.',rubric:[['Three mechanisms',['transformation','free dna','conjugation','pilus','transduction','phage']],['Selectable markers',['antibiotic','resistance','selective','ampicillin','streptomycin','marker']],['Skill II-A procedure',['mating plate','strain','loop','lb+amp','lb+str','lb+str+amp','sterile']],['Controls and interpretation',['confirmation','control','both','double','neither','growth']]],model:'Transformation is the uptake of free DNA, such as a plasmid, from the environment; conjugation is the direct transfer of a plasmid or chromosomal DNA from a donor with an F factor to a recipient through a pilus; and transduction is the transfer of bacterial DNA by a bacteriophage that packaged it during a previous infection. In Skill II-A, each E. coli strain carries a different antibiotic resistance marker, shown by the confirmation plates: for example, strain I grows on ampicillin but not streptomycin, and strain II grows on streptomycin but not ampicillin. These are the controls. Using sterile technique, I place drops of each strain on an LB mating plate, mix them with a new sterile loop, let the liquid soak in, invert and incubate, then streak cells from the mating plate onto LB+amp, LB+str and LB+str+amp plates. Because neither parent strain can grow on the double-antibiotic plate alone, colonies on LB+str+amp must contain both resistance genes, showing that genetic material transferred between the strains by conjugation.'},sources:[RULES,CAROLINA,OS_PROK,OS_BIOTECH]};

export const G9={title:'Test crosses with Drosophila and Fast Plants; chi-square (Skills III and V)',hook:'Mendel counted peas; you will sort sleeping fruit flies and pollinate tiny flowers with a wand. Then you will ask whether your counts fit the expected ratio—or whether the difference is too large to be chance. Skills III-A, III-B and V turn genetics into evidence.',objectives:['Anesthetize, sex and set up Drosophila crosses following Skill III-A.','Cross-pollinate Wisconsin Fast Plants, harvest seeds and plant the next generation following Skill III-B.','Perform a chi-square test following Skill V.','Decide whether data validate a proposed mode of inheritance.'],sections:[
['Working with Drosophila',[
'Fruit flies are handled briefly under anesthesia. **Sexing**: **females** are larger with a **pointed abdomen** marked by several dark stripes; **males** are smaller with a **rounded, dark-tipped abdomen** and **sex combs**—tiny dark bristles on the front legs. For controlled crosses, females must be **virgins**, collected within several hours after they emerge from the pupal case, because females store sperm. The life cycle—egg, three larval stages, pupa, adult—takes about **10–12 days at 25 °C**, so parents are removed before offspring emerge to avoid mixing generations. Flies are anesthetized with **FlyNap**, sorted with a soft **brush** on a white card and, if not needed, placed in a **morgue** (a container of oil or alcohol).',
sort('Male or female Drosophila?',['Male','Female'],[['Sex combs on the front legs',0],['Larger body',1],['Rounded, dark-tipped abdomen',0],['Pointed abdomen with stripes',1],['Must be a virgin for a controlled cross',1]])]],
['Setting up a Drosophila cross (Skill III-A)',[
'In Skill III-A (25 minutes), after closed-toed shoes and PPE, the competitor picks up the **F1 fly culture**, transfers flies to an **empty anesthetization vial** and inserts the plug, then inserts a **wand dipped in FlyNap** between the plug and the vial wall. When **all flies are sleeping**, they transfer them to a **sorting card**. They label two more cards **“Male (phenotype)”** and **“Female (phenotype)”**, **sort flies by sex with a sorting brush**, transfer **five males** and **five females** to the labeled cards and place the remaining flies in a **morgue**. For the cross, they gather **two culture vials, two plugs and two blank labels**, add **one level measure of Formula 4-24 Instant Drosophila Medium** and **one level measure of water** to each vial, sprinkle **four to six grains of yeast** on the medium (too much yeast can damage the culture), and **label each vial “F2,” the date and initials**. They clean and disinfect the work area and remove PPE (30 points; 70% is 21).',
order('Drosophila cross preparation (HOSA Skill III-A)',['PPE; transfer F1 flies to an anesthetization vial','Insert a FlyNap wand between plug and vial wall','When flies sleep, move them to a sorting card','Label male and female cards with phenotypes','Sort by sex; move 5 males and 5 females; morgue the rest','Add one measure of medium and one measure of water to two vials','Sprinkle 4–6 grains of yeast','Label vials F2 with date and initials; clean up'])]],
['Crossing Wisconsin Fast Plants (Skill III-B)',[
'Fast Plants flower about **two weeks** after planting; Skill III-B uses **13–16-day-old** plants. Because they are **self-incompatible**, pollen must come from a **different plant**. After PPE, the competitor uses a **pollination wand** to collect pollen from the **stamen** (anther) of the parent identified in the scenario as the **male** phenotype and applies it to the **pistil** (stigma) of flowers on the **female** parent. For **seed extraction**, they label a small envelope **“F1”** with the date and initials, fold a piece of paper lengthwise to make a crease and lay it flat, gently open the dried **seed pods** to collect seeds on the paper (removing non-seed parts) and funnel the seeds into the envelope. To **grow the next generation**, they moisten about **1 cup of soil with 35 mL of water**, soak a **wick** and place it through the precut X in the small container, add **⅓ cup of moist soil (about 1–1.5 cm)**, place **18 fertilizer pellets** evenly, add another **⅓ cup of soil (about 2.5–3 cm deep)**, space **10 seeds** evenly on the surface and cover them with a **thin layer (about 2 tablespoons) of moist soil**, set the small container in the rinsed large container (the reservoir), water gently with **50 mL**, add **one anti-algal square** and label the container **“F1,” the date and initials**, then clean up (30 points; 70% is 21).',
chk('Why can’t a Wisconsin Fast Plant be pollinated with its own pollen in Skill III-B?',['The plants are self-incompatible, so pollen must come from another plant','Its pollen is toxic','It has no stamens','The rating sheet requires two wands'],'Self-pollination does not produce seeds in these plants.')]],
['The chi-square test (Skill V)',[
'Counts rarely match expected ratios exactly. The **chi-square (χ²) goodness-of-fit test** asks whether the difference between **observed (o)** and **expected (e)** counts is small enough to be due to **chance**. Steps in Skill V: state the **experimental hypothesis** (a falsifiable prediction, such as “the traits are inherited as two unlinked genes with complete dominance”); construct a **null hypothesis** that refers to the independent and dependent variables (“there is no significant difference between the observed phenotype counts and those expected for a 9:3:3:1 ratio”); complete the **expected cross**; calculate **expected** counts by multiplying the total by each expected fraction; **score** the organisms and record **observed** counts; calculate **(o − e)**, **(o − e)²** and **(o − e)²/e** for each class; sum them to get **χ²** (the rating sheet calls this the calculated value); find **degrees of freedom = number of classes − 1**; and compare with the **critical value** at **p = 0.05** (3.84 for 1 df, 5.99 for 2 df, 7.81 for 3 df). If **χ² is less than the critical value**, the differences are likely due to chance and you **fail to reject (accept)** the null hypothesis; if it is greater, **reject** it. Finally, explain whether the mode of inheritance is **validated**.',
{t:'w',kind:'chisquare',ratio:'9:3:3:1',title:'Run a chi-square test',prompt:'Enter observed counts from an invented dihybrid cross and decide whether they fit 9:3:3:1.'}]],
['Interpreting results',[
'A χ² below the critical value does not **prove** the hypothesis—it means the data are **consistent** with it. A χ² above the critical value suggests another explanation, such as **linkage**, **epistasis**, **lethal alleles**, **sex linkage** or errors in scoring. Larger samples make the test more reliable, and expected counts should generally be at least about 5 per class. Always connect the statistical decision back to biology: “Because χ² = 2.4 is less than 7.81 at 3 degrees of freedom, I fail to reject the null hypothesis; the data support two independently assorting genes.”',
{t:'example',title:'A worked chi-square',prompt:'A dihybrid corn ear has 320 kernels: 186 purple smooth, 58 purple wrinkled, 60 yellow smooth and 16 yellow wrinkled. Do the data fit 9:3:3:1?',steps:['Expected: 320 × 9/16 = 180; 320 × 3/16 = 60; 60; 320 × 1/16 = 20.','(o − e)²/e: (6²)/180 = 0.20; (−2²)/60 = 0.07; (0²)/60 = 0; (−4²)/20 = 0.80.','χ² = 0.20 + 0.07 + 0 + 0.80 = 1.07; df = 4 − 1 = 3; critical value at p = 0.05 is 7.81.','1.07 < 7.81, so fail to reject the null hypothesis.'],answer:'The counts fit a 9:3:3:1 ratio, consistent with two independently assorting genes with complete dominance. (Counts are invented for practice.)'},
kase('A poor fit',{a:{text:'A dihybrid test cross expected to give 1:1:1:1 produces 420 and 410 parental-type offspring and 90 and 80 recombinant-type offspring (total 1,000). χ² is very large. What is the likely explanation?',choices:[['The genes are linked, so parental combinations are overrepresented','b','Correct. Linkage reduces recombinant classes.',true],['The genes assort independently','b','Independent assortment would give roughly equal classes.',false]]},b:{text:'What is the recombination frequency?',choices:[['17%, about 17 map units','c','Correct. (90 + 80) ÷ 1,000 = 0.17.',true],['83%','c','That is the parental frequency.',false]]},c:{text:'You reject the null hypothesis of independent assortment and propose that the genes are about 17 cM apart. (Counts are invented.)',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Drosophila: females larger with pointed striped abdomens, males with sex combs; FlyNap, sorting cards, 5 males + 5 females, medium + water + 4–6 yeast grains, label F2.','Fast Plants are self-incompatible: transfer pollen from the male parent’s stamen to the female parent’s pistil; harvest seeds; soil 35 mL water, wick, 18 pellets, 10 seeds, 50 mL water, anti-algal square.','Chi-square: expected = total × fraction; χ² = Σ(o − e)²/e; df = classes − 1; at p = 0.05 compare with 3.84, 5.99, 7.81; below the critical value, fail to reject the null.'],terms:[['Chi-square test','Statistical test of how well observed counts fit expected counts.'],['Degrees of freedom','Number of classes minus one in a goodness-of-fit test.'],['Self-incompatible','Unable to fertilize itself with its own pollen.']],mcqs:[q('Which feature identifies a male Drosophila?','Sex combs on the front legs','A pointed, striped abdomen','Larger size','Longer wings','Males also have rounded, dark-tipped abdomens.'),q('Why must females be virgins for a controlled Drosophila cross?','Females store sperm from earlier matings','Virgin females are larger','Males cannot mate with older females','It is not required','Stored sperm would confuse results.'),q('In Skill III-A, how many grains of yeast are added to each vial?','Four to six','A full teaspoon','None','Fifty','Too much yeast damages the culture.'),q('In Skill III-B, pollen is transferred from the:','Stamen of one plant to the pistil of another','Pistil to the stamen of the same flower','Leaf to the root','Seed to the soil','Fast Plants are self-incompatible.'),q('Degrees of freedom for a 9:3:3:1 chi-square test equal:','3','4','1','9','Four classes minus one.'),q('At p = 0.05 with 1 degree of freedom, the critical value is:','3.84','5.99','7.81','0.05','Compare χ² with this value.'),q('If χ² is less than the critical value, you:','Fail to reject (accept) the null hypothesis','Reject the null hypothesis','Prove the hypothesis','Repeat the test with fewer data','Differences are likely due to chance.'),q('Expected counts are calculated by:','Multiplying the total by each expected fraction','Copying the observed counts','Dividing observed by 2','Squaring the observed counts','For example, 320 × 9/16 = 180.')],frq:{prompt:'A student scores 400 F2 Fast Plants: 290 with purple stems and 110 with non-purple stems. Write the hypothesis and null hypothesis, perform a chi-square test for a 3:1 ratio, and explain whether the mode of inheritance is validated.',rubric:[['Hypotheses',['hypothesis','null','no significant difference','3:1','dominant']],['Expected values',['300','100','expected','400']],['Chi-square calculation',['(o-e)','0.33','1.0','1.33','χ²','chi']],['Degrees of freedom and decision',['1 degree','df','3.84','fail to reject','accept','validated']]],model:'Hypothesis: if purple stem color is controlled by one gene with purple dominant, then an F1 × F1 cross will produce purple and non-purple plants in a 3:1 ratio. Null hypothesis: there is no significant difference between the observed numbers of purple and non-purple plants (dependent variable) and the numbers expected for a 3:1 ratio from this cross (independent variable). Expected counts: 400 × ¾ = 300 purple and 400 × ¼ = 100 non-purple. For purple, (290 − 300)² ÷ 300 = 100 ÷ 300 ≈ 0.33; for non-purple, (110 − 100)² ÷ 100 = 1.00; χ² ≈ 1.33. With two classes, there is 1 degree of freedom, and the critical value at p = 0.05 is 3.84. Because 1.33 is less than 3.84, I fail to reject the null hypothesis: the difference is likely due to chance, so the data are consistent with—and validate—simple dominant inheritance of purple stem color, although they do not prove it. (Counts are invented for practice.)'},sources:[RULES,CAROLINA,FASTPLANTS]};

export const G10={title:'Population genetics, modern applications and careers',hook:'If 1 in 2,500 newborns has a recessive condition, about 1 in 25 people carries the allele without knowing it. Population genetics (10%) explains how allele frequencies behave and change, and modern applications and careers (10%) show how genetics now shapes medicine, agriculture and law.',objectives:['Use the Hardy-Weinberg equations to calculate allele and genotype frequencies.','Explain the conditions for equilibrium and the forces that change allele frequencies.','Describe modern applications of genetics, including testing, gene therapy and CRISPR, and their ethical issues.','Describe careers in genetics and their education paths.'],sections:[
['The Hardy-Weinberg principle',[
'A **population** shares a **gene pool**, and **allele frequencies** describe how common each allele is. The **Hardy-Weinberg principle** states that allele and genotype frequencies stay constant from generation to generation if no evolutionary forces act. For a gene with two alleles, **p + q = 1**, where p is the frequency of the dominant allele and q the recessive allele, and the genotype frequencies are **p² + 2pq + q² = 1**: **p²** homozygous dominant, **2pq** heterozygous and **q²** homozygous recessive. Because only the recessive phenotype reveals its genotype, problems usually start with **q²**. Example: if 9% of a population shows the recessive phenotype, q² = 0.09, so q = 0.3, p = 0.7, and carriers are 2pq = 0.42 (42%).',
{t:'w',kind:'hwlab',title:'Practice Hardy-Weinberg calculations',prompt:'Start from the recessive phenotype frequency, then find q and the carrier frequency.'}]],
['Conditions and forces of evolution',[
'Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium requires five conditions: **no mutation**, **random mating**, **no natural selection**, a **very large population** (no genetic drift) and **no gene flow** (migration). Real populations break these conditions, which causes **evolution**—a change in allele frequencies over time. **Mutation** creates new alleles. **Gene flow** moves alleles between populations. **Genetic drift** is random change, strongest in small populations, including the **bottleneck effect** (a disaster drastically reduces population size) and the **founder effect** (a few individuals start a new population, as in the high frequency of certain disorders in some isolated communities). **Nonrandom mating** changes genotype frequencies. **Natural selection** favors alleles that improve survival and reproduction; for example, the sickle cell allele is common where malaria is common because **heterozygotes** are protected against malaria (**heterozygote advantage**).',
match('Match the force to its example',[['Genetic drift (bottleneck)','A storm kills most of a small beetle population at random'],['Founder effect','A few settlers start an isolated community with a rare allele'],['Gene flow','Pollen blows from one field of plants to another'],['Natural selection','Antibiotic-resistant bacteria survive treatment'],['Mutation','A new allele appears from a DNA copying error'],['Heterozygote advantage','Sickle cell carriers resist malaria']])]],
['Genetic testing and medicine',[
'Genetics is transforming medicine. **Newborn screening** tests babies for treatable conditions such as phenylketonuria. **Carrier screening** tells prospective parents whether they carry recessive alleles. **Prenatal testing** includes noninvasive prenatal screening of fetal DNA in the mother’s blood and diagnostic tests such as amniocentesis. **Diagnostic** and **predictive** testing identify mutations linked to disease, such as *BRCA1/2* variants that raise breast and ovarian cancer risk. **Pharmacogenomics** uses a person’s genes to choose medications and doses. **Gene therapy** delivers working genes or edits faulty ones; **CRISPR-Cas9**, for which Jennifer Doudna and Emmanuelle Charpentier received the 2020 Nobel Prize, uses a guide RNA to direct the Cas9 enzyme to cut DNA at a precise site, and a CRISPR-based therapy for sickle cell disease was approved in the United States in 2023. The **Human Genome Project**, completed in 2003, mapped about 20,000 protein-coding genes.',
flip('Applications of genetics',[['Newborn screening','Detects treatable conditions like PKU soon after birth'],['Carrier screening','Shows whether parents carry recessive alleles'],['Pharmacogenomics','Matches medications to a person’s genes'],['CRISPR-Cas9','Guide RNA directs Cas9 to cut DNA at a precise site'],['DNA profiling','Compares short tandem repeats in forensics'],['GMO crops','Bt corn makes a protein toxic to certain insects']])]],
['Agriculture, forensics and ethics',[
'In **agriculture**, selective breeding and genetic engineering produce crops with higher yields, pest resistance (such as **Bt corn**) or added nutrients (such as Golden Rice with beta-carotene). **Cloning** produced Dolly the sheep in 1996. In **forensics**, **DNA profiling** compares **short tandem repeats (STRs)**—regions where short sequences repeat a variable number of times—between crime-scene samples and suspects, and databases such as CODIS store profiles. These powerful tools raise **ethical questions**: privacy of genetic data, informed consent, fair access to expensive therapies, editing embryos, and **genetic discrimination**. In the United States, the **Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act (GINA, 2008)** protects people from genetic discrimination in **health insurance and employment**, but not in life, disability or long-term care insurance.',
sort('Protected by GINA or not?',['Protected by GINA','Not covered by GINA'],[['Health insurance coverage decisions',0],['Hiring and firing decisions by employers',0],['Life insurance',1],['Long-term care insurance',1],['Disability insurance',1]])]],
['Careers in genetics',[
'Genetics careers span health care, research, agriculture and law. **Genetic counselors** help families understand genetic risks and test results; they earn a **master’s degree** in genetic counseling and certification from the **American Board of Genetic Counseling**, and the BLS projects much faster than average growth for the field. **Medical (clinical) geneticists** are physicians who diagnose and manage genetic disorders after residency training. **Clinical laboratory geneticists** and **cytogenetic and molecular technologists** perform karyotypes, DNA sequencing and other tests in clinical labs. **Research scientists** study genes in model organisms and humans, usually with a PhD. **Bioinformaticians** use computing to analyze genomic data. **Genetics nurses**, **plant and animal breeders**, **forensic DNA analysts** and **biotechnology** professionals also apply genetics daily. HOSA’s Genetics event, with its lab skills and test, mirrors the knowledge these careers use.',
match('Match the career to its work',[['Genetic counselor','Explains genetic risks and test results to families'],['Medical geneticist','Physician who diagnoses and manages genetic disorders'],['Cytogenetic technologist','Prepares and analyzes karyotypes'],['Bioinformatician','Analyzes large genomic datasets with software'],['Forensic DNA analyst','Compares STR profiles from evidence'],['Plant geneticist','Develops crops with improved traits']]),
kase('Counseling a family',{a:{text:'A couple learns they are both carriers of cystic fibrosis. What is the chance each pregnancy results in an affected child?',choices:[['25%','b','Correct. Aa × Aa gives a ¼ chance of aa.',true],['50%','b','That is the chance of a carrier child.',false]]},b:{text:'In their population, 1 in 2,500 newborns is affected. About what fraction of people are carriers?',choices:[['About 1 in 25 (2pq ≈ 0.04)','c','Correct. q² = 1/2,500, q = 0.02, 2pq ≈ 0.039.',true],['About 1 in 2,500','c','That is the affected (q²) frequency.',false]]},c:{text:'A genetic counselor explains these risks, testing options and support resources so the couple can make informed decisions.',end:true}})]],
["Natural selection and speciation",["Natural selection acts on phenotypes and shifts allele frequencies in three main patterns. **Directional selection** favors one extreme, such as antibiotic resistance spreading through a bacterial population or darker moths during industrial pollution. **Stabilizing selection** favors intermediate phenotypes, such as human birth weight. **Disruptive selection** favors both extremes, such as seed-cracking birds with either small or large beaks. **Sexual selection** favors traits that improve mating success. Over long periods, these changes can contribute to **speciation**, the formation of new species, when populations become **reproductively isolated**—for example, by geographic barriers (**allopatric speciation**) or by changes in timing or behavior.","Population genetics links genes to evolution: Hardy-Weinberg provides the “null hypothesis” of no evolution, and measuring how real populations depart from it reveals which forces are acting. Medical genetics uses the same thinking to estimate carrier frequencies, plan screening programs and track how resistance alleles spread in pathogens.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the type of selection to its example","pairs":[["Directional selection","Bacteria become increasingly antibiotic-resistant"],["Stabilizing selection","Average birth weights have the highest survival"],["Disruptive selection","Birds with very small or very large beaks survive best"],["Sexual selection","Bright plumage attracts mates"],["Allopatric speciation","A mountain range separates two populations"],["Reproductive isolation","Populations can no longer interbreed"]]}]]
],takeaways:['p + q = 1 and p² + 2pq + q² = 1; start from q² (recessive phenotype); carriers (2pq) far outnumber affected individuals for rare traits.','Equilibrium needs no mutation, random mating, no selection, a large population and no gene flow; drift, founder and bottleneck effects, gene flow and selection change allele frequencies.','Applications include screening, pharmacogenomics, gene therapy, CRISPR, GMOs and STR profiling; GINA protects health insurance and employment; careers include genetic counselor and medical geneticist.'],terms:[['Allele frequency','Proportion of a particular allele in a gene pool.'],['Genetic drift','Random change in allele frequencies, strongest in small populations.'],['Short tandem repeat','DNA region with a variable number of short repeats, used in profiling.']],mcqs:[q('If q² = 0.16, what is q?','0.4','0.16','0.84','0.6','q = √0.16.'),q('In Hardy-Weinberg, 2pq represents:','Heterozygotes (carriers)','Homozygous dominant individuals','Homozygous recessive individuals','The recessive allele frequency','p² and q² are homozygotes.'),q('Which condition is required for Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?','Random mating','Natural selection','Small population size','Frequent migration','Also no mutation and no gene flow.'),q('A few individuals found a new isolated population with a rare allele. This is:','The founder effect','Gene flow','Natural selection','The bottleneck effect','It is a type of genetic drift.'),q('CRISPR-Cas9 uses which molecule to find its target?','A guide RNA','A ribosome','A restriction plasmid','A tRNA anticodon','Cas9 then cuts the DNA.'),q('GINA protects against genetic discrimination in:','Health insurance and employment','Life insurance','Long-term care insurance','Disability insurance','Passed in 2008.'),q('Genetic counselors typically earn a:','Master’s degree in genetic counseling','High school diploma only','Medical degree','Law degree','Certified by the ABGC.'),q('The sickle cell allele remains common where malaria is common because of:','Heterozygote advantage','Genetic drift only','Gene flow from cold climates','Random mating','Carriers resist malaria.')],frq:{prompt:'In a population, 4% of individuals show a recessive trait. Use Hardy-Weinberg to find all allele and genotype frequencies, state the assumptions, and explain two forces that could change these frequencies over time.',rubric:[['Calculations',['0.04','0.2','0.8','0.64','0.32']],['Equations',['p + q = 1','p² + 2pq + q²','p2','2pq','q²']],['Assumptions',['random mating','no mutation','no selection','large population','no gene flow','migration']],['Forces of change',['drift','founder','bottleneck','selection','gene flow','mutation']]],model:'The recessive phenotype frequency is q² = 0.04, so q = √0.04 = 0.2 and p = 1 − 0.2 = 0.8. Genotype frequencies are p² = 0.64 homozygous dominant, 2pq = 2 × 0.8 × 0.2 = 0.32 heterozygous carriers and q² = 0.04 homozygous recessive, which add to 1 (p + q = 1 and p² + 2pq + q² = 1). These calculations assume the population is at equilibrium: no mutation, random mating, no natural selection, a very large population with no genetic drift and no gene flow. If the population were small, genetic drift—such as a bottleneck from a disaster or a founder effect when a few individuals start a new colony—could change allele frequencies by chance. If the recessive phenotype reduced survival or reproduction, natural selection would lower q over time, while gene flow from another population with different allele frequencies would also shift p and q.'},sources:[RULES,OS_POP,NHGRI,GINA,BLS_GC]};
